ide additional nucleation potential\",\"explanation\":\"Deformation bands can provide ferrite-nucleation potential equivalent to that of austenite grain boundaries. Their importance is reflected in the description of α-nucleation within unrecrystallized γ-grains, where ferrite can form on deformation bands as well as at γ-grain boundaries. [Data: Relationships (1583, 267); Entities (248)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Conventional hot rolling limits nucleation locations\",\"explanation\":\"Conventional hot rolling is described as a high-temperature process in which ferrite nucleates exclusively at austenite grain boundaries. The associated conventional hot-rolled steel serves as a comparison material and is characterized by the same boundary-restricted nucleation behavior. This restriction provides fewer opportunities for intragranular nucleation and limits the extent of grain refinement. [Data: Entities (52, 11); Relationships (11, 42)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Controlled-rolled steel extends nucleation into the grain interior\",\"explanation\":\"In controlled-rolled steel, ferrite nucleation occurs both at austenite grain boundaries and within the grain interior. The grain interior may contain deformation bands that act as additional nucleation sites, contrasting with the boundary-exclusive behavior described for conventional hot-rolled and normalized steels. [Data: Entities (47, 62, 248); Relationships (38, 267)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Partial recrystallization creates a mixed microstructural region\",\"explanation\":\"Partial recrystallization produces fine recrystallized grains preferentially near γ-grain boundaries, while recovered grains coexist with recrystallized grains in a mixed structure. At the same time, an unrecrystallized region can remain within the grain interior, linking interior microstructure to the potential for ferrite nucleation in controlled-rolled steel. [Data: Entities (386, 314, 383, 384); Relationships (486, 489, 490)]\"}],\"rating\":7.0,\"rating_explanation\":